# Expectation record for 04_bitfields/004_signed_unsigned.c
#
# Reproduce one cell by hand with no harness: render the three command templates
# below, substituting the target triple, the optimization level, the source path,
# the output path, and the target's runner (empty on the natively executing target).

program            = 004_signed_unsigned
area               = 04_bitfields
description        = Narrow signed bitfields at their exact minimum and unsigned bitfields at their exact maximum, folded, volatile runtime and derived variants
targets            = x86_64, i686, aarch64, riscv64
opt_levels         = -O0, -O1, -O2
shared_flags       = -static
bcc_command        = $BCC --target <triple> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
ref_command        = $REF_CC_<TRIPLE> <opt> -static <src> -o <out>
run_command        = <runner> <out>
expect_exit        = 0
oracle_a           = enabled
oracle_b           = enabled
oracle_c           = enabled
ub_notes           <<END
The default undefined-behaviour warning gate applies unmodified, with no deviation:
-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -Wconversion -Wsign-conversion -Wshadow -Werror.  The program
was verified to pass that gate and to run cleanly under
-fsanitize=undefined,address -fno-sanitize-recover=all.

Every value stored is exactly representable in its destination field, so no conversion can
change a value: each signed field receives its own minimum or maximum, and each unsigned field
receives its own maximum, computed from the field's declared width.  The two widest signed
minima are spelled as -1073741823 - 1 and -2147483647 - 1 so that the constant remains an int
throughout; writing the minimum as a single negated literal would make it a long, because the
positive form does not fit an int.

The derived block computes each extreme at run time from an opaque volatile seed rather than
storing a constant.  Unsigned destinations are masked to their exact field width, so the
conversion into the field is provably value-preserving.  Signed destinations select between two
literals that both lie inside the field, so no out-of-range conversion into a signed bitfield
can occur; such a conversion would be implementation-defined and is therefore avoided rather
than relied upon.  The multiplications in the derived block operate on int values no larger
than 65535 and cannot overflow.

No shift operator appears anywhere in the program, so no question of shift range, negative
left shift or implementation-defined negative right shift arises.  No aliasing violation: no
pointer is formed to any bitfield and no type-punning occurs.  No uninitialized read: the
folded objects have explicit constant initializers, and the volatile objects and the seed have
static storage duration and so are zero-initialized, and in any case every field is written
before it is read.  No object is modified twice between sequence points.  No pointer value,
address, timestamp, random value or locale-dependent format is printed.
END
impl_defined_notes <<END
Every bitfield is declared with an explicit signed int or unsigned int base type.  A plain
int bitfield's signedness is implementation-defined, so using it would manufacture a spurious
divergence; stating the signedness in the declaration removes that dependency entirely.  That
choice is what makes this program meaningful at all, since its entire subject is the exact
minimum of a signed field and the exact maximum of an unsigned field.

Plain char is not used.  Plain char signedness was measured to differ across the four targets
(signed on x86_64 and i686, unsigned on aarch64 and riscv64), so it is avoided entirely.

No value whose width varies by target is printed.  sizeof(long) and sizeof(void *) differ on
i686 (4) from the other three targets (8), so neither is printed, and no pointer value or
address appears in the output.  Every bitfield is declared on an int base type, and int is
four bytes on all four targets, so a 32-bit-wide field is exactly full width everywhere and
all fourteen extremes are target-invariant by derivation.  Every printf argument is explicitly
cast to unsigned or int.

Three independent routes reach the same extremes and the expected output asserts they agree:
a constant initializer folded at compile time, a constant store into volatile storage, and a
value computed at run time from an opaque volatile seed.  A disagreement between those routes
would localize a defect to the constant folder or to the backend respectively.

All four targets and all three oracles remain enabled.  The minimum value of a narrow signed
bitfield was measured identical on all four targets, so a cross-backend divergence here is a
genuine finding rather than an implementation-defined difference.
END
expected_stdout    <<END
folded_smin s2=-2 s3=-4 s5=-16 s8=-128 s16=-32768 s31=-1073741824 s32=-2147483648
folded_smax s2=1 s3=3 s5=15 s8=127 s16=32767 s31=1073741823 s32=2147483647
folded_umax u1=1 u2=3 u5=31 u8=255 u16=65535 u31=2147483647 u32=4294967295
runtime_smin s2=-2 s3=-4 s5=-16 s8=-128 s16=-32768 s31=-1073741824 s32=-2147483648
runtime_smax s2=1 s3=3 s5=15 s8=127 s16=32767 s31=1073741823 s32=2147483647
runtime_umax u1=1 u2=3 u5=31 u8=255 u16=65535 u31=2147483647 u32=4294967295
derived_smin s2=-2 s5=-16 s8=-128 s16=-32768
derived_umax u1=1 u5=31 u8=255 u16=65535
near_extreme s5=-15 u5=30
END
